; spfall.mail.test
;
; A single fault, isolated: SPF that authorizes the entire internet. Present as its own domain so
; a chain can assert on the qualifier without any other finding in the way.
;
; Addresses come from the RFC 5737 documentation range and the domain is under .test, which
; RFC 2606 reserves, so nothing here can route anywhere real.

$ORIGIN spfall.mail.test.
$TTL 60

@       IN  SOA  ns1.spfall.mail.test. admin.spfall.mail.test. (
                 2026092401 ; serial
                 3600       ; refresh
                 900        ; retry
                 604800     ; expire
                 60 )       ; minimum

@       IN  NS   ns1.spfall.mail.test.
ns1     IN  A    127.0.0.1

@       IN  A    192.0.2.50
mail    IN  A    192.0.2.51
@       IN  MX   10 mail.spfall.mail.test.

; +all passes every sender, so the record is worse than no record: it tells a receiver that the
; whole internet is authorized, and a checker looking only for "does an SPF record exist" reports
; the domain protected.
@       IN  TXT  "v=spf1 +all"

; ?all on a subdomain, the quieter version of the same thing. Neutral is treated as no policy at
; all by most receivers, so it is worth a separate finding from ~all softfail.
neutral IN  TXT  "v=spf1 ip4:192.0.2.51 ?all"
